cff-version: 1.2.0
title: neurostim-safety
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it. Please also cite the primary
  sources for whichever limits your work relies on -- every constant in the
  package carries its own citation, retrievable with
  neurostim.bibliography().
type: software
# ORCID, DOI and release date are intentionally absent: none has been assigned, and
# none is invented (ledger 180). Add them here when they exist.
repository-code: https://github.com/kshtjkumar/neurostim-safety
authors:
  - given-names: Kshitij
    family-names: Kumar
    affiliation: Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, India
abstract: >-
  Electrode safety and stimulation modelling for neural interfaces. Every
  literature-derived constant carries its primary source and the conditions
  under which it was measured; charge-injection limits are pulse-width,
  polarity, electrolyte and temperature dependent, and the package records
  those conditions rather than discarding them. Includes the Shannon
  tissue-damage criterion with the envelope of its fitting data enforced,
  material charge-injection limits, water-window and chronic-degradation
  checks, the microelectrode charge-per-phase regime, Pennes bioheat modelling
  that, given the drive of Elwassif et al. (2006), returns a peak rise of
  0.83 K against the 0.82 K of their finite element model, and a suite that
  reproduces published results from published inputs.
keywords:
  - neurostimulation
  - electrode
  - electrochemistry
  - deep brain stimulation
  - charge injection
  - Shannon criterion
  - neural engineering
license: MIT
version: 0.16.0
